  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "The loss is exactly L = (2.5 x^2 + z^2) / 2, drawn at height 0.5 L over x, z in [-2, 2]. It is steeper along x, so overshooting starts there once learning rate > 0.4 and swings grow without end above 0.8.",
    "Each step moves the ball by -learning rate × (true slope + noise). The noise is Gaussian, centred on zero, with spread 2·sqrt((64−B)/(63·B)) for batch size B, so it is exactly zero at 64.",
    "Both arrows are drawn at 2× the true step length, the same scale for each. The orange arrow is the step the ball takes next. The grey one is -learning rate × true slope. The step-length readout gives the true length.",
    "Diverged means the loss went above 5 or the ball left the drawn bowl. The run stops there, the ball is drawn at the bowl's rim, and the loss readout keeps its true value. A run also stops after 40 steps (finished).",
    "While playing, the ball takes 5 updates per second. 'Step once' makes exactly one update. Noise comes from a fixed seed, and a new run continues the noise sequence. Reset replays it from the start and clears the ghost path.",
    "With noise, a single step lowers the loss only on average. Small batches can raise it on a given step."
  ]
}